{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Isaac sent, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>the brother of Rebekah</b> — who does not know this? — especially since it says so nearby. And further, its saying <b>the mother of Jacob and Esau</b> — are these superfluous words?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the verse comes to give the reason for our father Isaac sending a righteous son to a notorious wicked man, Laban the Aramean. And although Abraham sent and took Rebekah, Bethuel was not notorious for wickedness like Laban. And further, Abraham did not explicitly say to take Bethuel's daughter, whereas Isaac explicitly commanded Jacob to take a wife from the daughters of Laban the wicked. Therefore it says <b>the brother of Rebekah</b> — both of them came forth from Bethuel the wicked, and likewise a righteous daughter will come forth from Laban. Also, one cannot prove that a chain of the root of holiness will not emerge from a wicked man — therefore it says <b>the mother of Jacob and Esau</b>, meaning: behold, the chain of holiness emerged from a wicked father; likewise one should not conclude and say that since Laban is wicked, no daughters like Rebekah will emerge from him." "<b>And you should know</b> that specifically in those days, when the holy souls had not yet been extracted from the place of their captivity, the family of Abraham was recognized as the place where holiness had acquired for itself a dwelling, and Abraham and Sarah were an everlasting sign, for they were the first converts, and whoever would convert from the nations at any time would be called Abraham, and a female, Sarah. Therefore the species of holiness seeks after its own kind, for Abraham was one alone and the structure of holiness was not completed in males and females, for he begot only Isaac and did not beget females together with him. And perhaps this is why the verse says (Genesis 25:19): ״אַבְרָהָם הוֹלִיד אֶת יִצְחָק״ — meaning, he alone, without a female counterpart; and likewise Isaac had no daughters, for the female sparks of holiness were still in the land of captivity — therefore Isaac sent him to search after his lost property." "<b>But</b> this is the day we have hoped for, for the root of holiness has sprouted — they are the children of Israel — and the staff of majesty and splendor has flowered, and the tree of life has been recognized, the portion of HaShem, His people Jacob, the lot of His inheritance; they are the ones radiant in holiness. And no man will turn aside from the tribes of Israel to the families of the earth. And if any sparks of holiness remained among the nations, behold, upon their coming forth into the world they gather themselves and return, and after their conversion they are believed, for they were lost — and they are ours." "<b>And the reason for</b> the Canaanite, concerning whom Abraham was insistent not to take a wife from them, and Isaac likewise commanded Jacob concerning this — our Rabbis said (Genesis Rabbah, chapter 59) that he is cursed and the seed of Abraham is blessed, etc. — and their words are sealed. But according to what I have written, we find the core reason to be correct, for Abraham's reason is that he (the Canaanite) is denuded of holy souls and all his daughters are an evil and abominable portion; therefore they resolved not to take from him, for no holy thing will be found in him. And this is the sod of its saying (Genesis 9:25): ״אָרוּר כְּנָעַן״ — meaning: utterly denuded of the species of holiness." "<b>And you will find</b> that from among the nations until this day converts still gather and come to take shelter in the shade of holiness — something we have not heard from days of old, that a Canaanite convert [should come] like the rest of the peoples, and this will confirm our words. And perhaps it is for this reason that the El commanded (Deuteronomy 20:16) ״לֹא תְחַיֶּה כָּל נְשָׁמָה״ — you shall not let live any soul — from among the seven nations, and He was precise to say ״כָּל נְשָׁמָה״ to give a reason for the commandment of ״לֹא תְחַיֶּה״, for there is no נְשָׁמָה in them — meaning, a holy soul to which the name נְשָׁמָה could be ascribed, so as to have compassion upon it and let it live, as with Ammon and Moab. Understand this." "<b>And after</b> Elohim has made all this known to us, we need to set our hearts to [ask] why it is that because of our sins we are subjugated to Canaan (Judges 4:2), after we know with certainty that the subjugation is for the purpose of refining the holy sparks that are within the klipah, while in the Canaanites there is no holy spark to descend after in exile. And you will find that Egypt, which had been emptied of the sparks, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Pesachim 118a) — that Israel did not leave until they had made it like a sieve that has no grain and like a pond that has no fish, as it is written ״וַיְנַצְּלוּ אֶת מִצְרָיִם״ (Exodus 12:36) — HaShem commanded not to return there, as the reason is explained in the words of the Arizal of blessed memory (Sha'ar HaGilgulim). If so, how could it be that we should endure the entire burden of this exile when there are no sparks of holiness in it?" "<b>Now,</b> the matter may be explained according to what they of blessed memory said (Likutei Torah, Mikeitz), that there are two kinds of holy sparks imprisoned in the klipah: the first are precious souls that come forth through the medium of birth, such as Abraham, Sarah, Rebecca, and Rachel, and the soul of Rabbi Chanina ben Teradyon that went forth from Shechem with Dinah, and Ruth from Moab and Na'amah from Ammon and Onkelos the convert and the like. The second is a kind of spark of holiness that is attached to the klipah and does not come forth at all, but is sunk within the klipah, and it has no possibility of separating from it except when Israel suffers through distress or through blows, for through this the holiness becomes separated and returns to its root. And from now on there is a reason for this exile, for there is a desire to refine the aspect of the holy sparks that do not separate except through the distress that Israel suffers. And concerning this exile Scripture said (Obadiah 1:20) ״וְגָלוּת הַחֵל הַזֶּה לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר כְּנַעֲנִים עַד צָרְפַת״ — that Scripture called it הַחֵל from the language of ״נִהְיֵיתִי וְנַחֲלֵיתִי״ (Daniel 8:27), for the attainment of the purpose of the exile among them is through the medium of distress, and therefore it is different from all the other exiles, as we have seen with our own eyes. And it said ״אֲשֶׁר כְּנַעֲנִים״ and did not say ״בַּכְּנַעֲנִים״, and also said ״כְּנַעֲנִים״ in the plural — perhaps this alludes to the holy sparks, which because they are now within the klipah of Canaan are called by its name כְּנַעֲנִים. And the reading of the verse along this line is: גָּלוּת הַחֵל הַזֶּה — this exile which is the exile of Israel — is for the sake of בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל who are now called כְּנַעֲנִים on account of the place in which they are."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {7 "The Talmudic citation from Daniel 8:27 reads 'נִהְיֵיתִי וְנַחֲלֵיתִי' in some witnesses and 'נִהְיֵיתִי וְנַחֲלֵית' in others; the text appears to follow the former reading. The phrase הַחֵל is understood here as related to חוֹלִי (illness/distress), drawn from the Daniel verse where the root conveys being faint and ill."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:38.662587927Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}